## What's here

| File | Chapter / section | Status |
|---|---|---|
| `1.2.1-sets.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.1 Set Theory | ✅ full transliteration; set algebra + De Morgan (hand proofs) + §1.2 exercises via `ext` (19 theorems), kernel-checked |
| `1.2.2-functions.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.2 Cartesian Products and Mappings | ✅ full transliteration; composition associativity/preservation + invertible⇒bijective forward + the (g∘f)⁻¹ = f⁻¹∘g⁻¹ exercise (11 theorems), kernel-checked; bijective⇒invertible marked (needs a choice operator) |
| `1.2.3-relations.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.3 Equivalence Relations and Partitions | ◑ FOL-tractable part; the three equivalence-projections + the "reflexivity is redundant" exercise (4 theorems), kernel-checked |
| `1.2.3-partitions.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.3 Equivalence Classes and Partitions | ◑ the tractable heart via `classOf(x): Set` — classes nonempty, [x]=[y] iff x∼y, equal-or-disjoint (3 theorems, kernel-checked); the "form a partition" packaging (a set of sets) stays a `hole` (needs `--draft`; sets-of-sets deferred) |
| `3.2-groups.md` | 3 — Groups, §3.2 Definitions and Examples | ✅ full transliteration; definition + 7 examples + basic-properties propositions (10 theorems) + Exercise 31, verified pure |

The set theory rests on `std/set.bpa` (sets over an abstract `Universe` of
elements, fixed membershipwise, with extensionality). Every identity is proved
by the same recipe — extensionality → fix an element → unfold `member(x, ·)` →
one `[by tautology …]` — so every step in the chapter is kernel-checked.

The function theory rests on `std/function.bpa` (maps as opaque `Fn` objects
over a `Universe`, with `apply`/`compose`, an identity, and
injective/surjective/invertible, fixed by their axioms plus **function
extensionality**). Composition associativity is a funcExt rewrite chain; the
preservation and invertibility results are FOL over the defining predicates,
with existential witnesses (`g(b)` for onto, the unpacked inverse for
invertible⇒bijective).

The group theory rests on `std/group.bpa` (the group axioms as a reusable bpa
theory) and uses the `assoc` tactic (associativity-only reordering) for the
non-commutative rearrangements.

## What's deferred (the forcing-function backlog)

Most of the textbook needs machinery bpa does not yet have. Rather than skip
those results silently, each is kept in the relevant `.md` with an in-place note
(`**Deferred:** …` for an exercise, or a `// this section cannot currently be
encoded [reason]` block for a stated theorem) saying exactly what it would
require — a running backlog of standard-library constructions that future
chapters will motivate:

- a concrete **ℤ_n / U(n)** model (modular arithmetic as a group carrier),
- **finite sets + cardinality** (counting arguments, Cayley tables, Lagrange),
- **matrix / GLₙ** constructions and the reals,
- a **group power** `gⁿ` (recursive over a Nat exponent) for the ℕ-indexed
  exercises,
- **sets-of-sets / quotient structure + a relation sort** — Chapter 1's §1.2.3
  (equivalence relations ⟺ partitions) needs equivalence classes `[a]` and the
  partition `X = ⋃[x]`, which `std/set.bpa`'s flat `member : Universe → Set`
  model cannot express; and the **indexed families** `⋃ᵢ Aᵢ` / `⋂ᵢ Aᵢ` need a
  sort of finite sequences of `Set`,
- a **choice / inverse-construction operator** — Chapter 1's §1.2.2 backward
  direction (bijective ⇒ invertible) builds the inverse by "letting `g(b) = a`"
  for the unique preimage `a`; that definite description (turning "for each `b`
  a unique `a`" into a function value) is not expressible in bpa's first-order
  logic, so `invertibleDef`'s `exists g` cannot be discharged,
- a **pair sort `Universe × Universe`** (with a set-of-pairs theory) for the
  §1.2.2 Cartesian products `A × B` and the pairs-based definition of a
  function; `std/function.bpa` sidesteps it by taking `apply(f, x)` primitive.

Each is a self-contained addition; the translation grows as they land.
